  "brief_md": "# Evidence Brief: P10 — Marketplace for Buying/Selling Small Startups\n\n## Idea\nA marketplace connecting buyers and sellers of small startups (micro-SaaS, indie apps, side projects), targeting micro-SaaS founders as sellers and small-business acquirers as buyers.\n\n## Payer\nThe dominant model across comparables is **seller-pays-at-close**: a success/closing fee taken as a percentage of sale price, sometimes plus a small recurring listing fee while live. Acquire.com's published structure — $25-$100/mo listing fee, plus 8%/7%/6% closing fee depending on deal size — is the clearest anchor ([acquire.com/seller-pricing](https://acquire.com/seller-pricing/)). Some competitors instead charge buyers a subscription for premium access/contact rights, or charge sellers a flat one-time listing fee with 0% commission (IndieExit, BuyMicroStartups).\n\n## Competitors (5 direct, all already live)\n| Name | Pricing | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Acquire.com | $25-100/mo listing + 6-8% closing fee | https://acquire.com/seller-pricing/ |\n| Flippa | $29+ listing, tiered success fee 5-10% | https://flippa.com/pricing |\n| MicroExits | 0% upfront, 5% success fee on close | https://microexits.com/ |\n| IndieExit | 0% commission for sellers, buyer premium membership | https://indieexit.com/about-us/ |\n| BuyMicroStartups | Free to list, flat one-time fee instead of commission | https://www.buymicrostartups.com/ |\n\nNotably, a near-identical new entrant (DealMyApp) launched in 2026 explicitly positioning against Acquire/Flippa as 'too big-deal-focused,' charging 0% commission — evidence the niche is already being actively re-entered by solo builders ([Indie Hackers](https://www.indiehackers.com/post/i-built-a-marketplace-for-indie-apps-two-weeks-in-positioning-is-harder-than-building-it-bc13757c9a)).\n\n## Acquisition Channels\n- **Indie Hackers newsletter sponsorship**: ~$750 for a one-time placement to ~80k subscribers at ~1.6% advertised CTR; a documented case produced 124 clicks and **zero sales** ([isora.me](https://www.isora.me/email-newsletter-sponsorship/)).\n- **Product Hunt launch**: free, used by competitor BuyMicroStartups for initial exposure ([Product Hunt](https://www.producthunt.com/products/buymicrostartups)).\n\n## Demand Evidence\n- \"I didn't have an audience. I didn't know where serious buyers were.\" — founder trying to sell a side project ([Indie Hackers](https://www.indiehackers.com/post/i-realized-building-software-was-easy-selling-it-wasnt-f7d30e1be3))\n- \"I'm seeing about 500+ unique visitors a day with only about 30 live listings... Buyers are slower to convert than sellers are to list.\" — operator of a directly competing marketplace two weeks post-launch ([Indie Hackers](https://www.indiehackers.com/post/i-built-a-marketplace-for-indie-apps-two-weeks-in-positioning-is-harder-than-building-it-bc13757c9a))\n\n## Case For\nA solo founder with AI agents could replicate the labor-intensive, human 'acquisition expert' services Acquire.com uses to justify its fees (listing optimization, buyer vetting, deal coaching — [acquire.com/seller-pricing](https://acquire.com/seller-pricing/)) at near-zero marginal cost, and could underprice or out-service incumbents in a narrow niche (e.g., sub-$10k deals) that bigger platforms don't prioritize. A single developer has already proven the build is feasible in weeks (DealMyApp, Laravel, solo dev).\n\n## Case Against\nAt least five direct competitors already operate, three with 0% or near-0% commission, so price is not a viable differentiator. The documented failure mode is not supply (listings) but buyer-side liquidity and trust — the closest comparable (DealMyApp) reports healthy traffic and listings but weak buyer conversion just two weeks in. A real-world $750 paid-acquisition test aimed at a similar audience produced zero sales. Incumbents (Acquire.com's 500k+ buyer network, Flippa's decade-plus SEO) own the buyer-trust moat that a one-person site would need significant time to replicate — classic two-sided marketplace cold-start risk that marketing spend alone has not solved for comparable players.\n\n## Open Questions\n1. Can one operator reach critical mass of *active* buyers before sellers churn to a free competitor?\n2. Would a sharper niche (vertical, deal-size band) reduce direct competition enough to build liquidity?\n3. What fee structure will users tolerate given 0%-commission rivals already exist?\n4. What realistic listing-to-close conversion rate and sales-cycle length should be assumed?\n5. Can escrow/KYC/financial verification be fully outsourced to avoid money-handling risk?\n6. Is a narrower wedge (curated dealflow newsletter, AI buyer-matching layer on existing listings) more viable than a new standalone marketplace?\n\n## Regulatory Setup\n- **Escrow/money flow**: must route all funds through a licensed third-party escrow provider to avoid money-transmitter licensing (one-time legal consult; ongoing vendor relationship) — precedent: MicroExits uses a licensed escrow provider (https://microexits.com/).\n- **Business broker licensing**: generally not required for a pure listings marketplace in most U.S. states, but varies by state and by how much deal-negotiation involvement the platform provides (one-time state-by-state legal check) (https://www.websiteclosers.com/resources/does-it-really-matter-that-business-brokers-are-not-licensed/).\n- **Data handling** of sensitive financial/identity data: ongoing obligation, no specific sourced regulation identified in this pass — flagged as unsourced.\n"
